---
// The architecture, drawn: above, OpenTofu as you run it today (one command, one process, one
// pass, one statefile); below, Turf as the same pieces run as a service (turf-mcp-server,
// restate-server, the console, turf-engine replicas, a round, flow control). Ported 2026-11-08
// from page 5 of the KubeCon NA 2026 one-pagers (headquarters marketing/launch/kubecon-onepagers.html,
// v0.3). Fills and strokes are tokens (global.css, "figures"), so it holds in dark mode. Flow
// control is drawn dashed: not yet shipped. Few words on purpose; the caption carries the prose.
---

<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 720 706" role="img" aria-label="Two panels. Above, OpenTofu as you run it: a person runs tofu plan and tofu apply; one process parses, builds the graph and walks it in one pass, asks for a yes at the terminal, and exits; state is one file in a bucket under one lock; provider plugins are child processes over gRPC, each reaching a cloud: AWS, Google Cloud, a Kubernetes cluster. Below, Turf as the same pieces run as a service: an agent over MCP, a person and a CI job reach turf-mcp-server, which parses the same configuration and writes the same statefile to the same bucket; restate-server keeps the durable journal, which the console reads; turf-engine runs as replicas, each refreshing, planning and applying per resource, driven by a round that plans, waits at an approval point, applies, and plans again until nothing is deferred; every call fans out through a per-provider flow-control valve, drawn dashed because it is not yet shipped, to one provider instance per region, account or alias, reaching the same three clouds.">
